# PART II — DESIGN & RATIONALE

## §D1 Problem B — per-round message identity (the model)

**Decision:** the *round* (one assistant streaming turn between user/tool interactions) is the unit of a UI bubble. Each round gets a server-minted `message_id` (uuid4 hex, short). This id is the single source of truth for "which bubble does this content belong to".

**Why server-minted, not UI-derived:** only the server knows where a round begins (it controls the agentic loop and the `message_start` emit). A UI heuristic ("new bubble when content resets") is fragile — it already failed here (B-F5). The server emitting the boundary id is the correct, deterministic design.

**Lifecycle:**
```
round begins  → server: message_start {message_id}
streaming     → server: token {message_id, content}
round ends:
  • normal    → server: message_end {message_id, final_content, turn_number}
  • needs input → server: pending_input {message_id, input_mode, ...}
```
The UI keeps a `currentMessageId`. On `message_start` it opens a new streaming bubble keyed by that id. `token` appends to the bubble with the matching id. `message_end`/`pending_input` commit/finalize that id's bubble. A subsequent `message_start` with a **new** id opens a **new** bubble — round 1's bubble is already committed and untouched.

## §D2 Problem B — why this also unblocks Problem A

A tabbed group (Problem A) is attached to a `pending_input` round. If that round shares a buffer with the preceding text round (today's bug), the text round's content is lost when the tabbed widget arrives. **Per-round identity (B) is therefore a prerequisite for A** — only once each round is its own bubble can "preamble text bubble" + "tabbed-tools bubble" coexist. This is why the plan does B first.

## §D3 Problem A — `parallel_group` semantics (the contract)

A new optional integer field on each emitted tool: `parallel_group`.

- **Same `parallel_group` value** ⇒ those tools may be shown **together** (one tabbed compound widget) and are answered in one submit.
- **Different values** ⇒ tools run **in sequence** (group 0 fully resolved before group 1 is shown), each as its own round.
- **Omitted `parallel_group`** ⇒ **consecutive tools coalesce** into one implicit group (the friendly default — improves existing SOPs without edits). The moment a tool declares an explicit group, the explicit grouping takes over for that response.

This is purely *expressive*: it lets the SOP author say "ask these two questions side-by-side" vs "ask Q1, then based on the answer, ask Q2".

**Why integer, not bool:** a bool ("parallel: true") can't express *more than one* sequential group in a single response (e.g. group 0 = [path, artifacts], then group 1 = [confirm]). An ordered integer can.

## §D4 Problem A — rendering (the widget)

- Server sets `metadata.widget_type = "compound_tabbed"` (in addition to the existing `metadata.compound = true`) when a group has >1 tool, so the UI dispatcher (`widget_type || mode`, A-F5) routes to the new widget without disturbing other modes.
- New React component `CompoundTabbedInput`: renders one MUI `Tab` per tool in `metadata.tools`, each tab hosting the *existing* per-tool widget (clarification → TextInput/PathAutocomplete; single_choice → SingleChoiceWidget; etc.). A single **Submit** gathers every tab's value into the compound payload.
- A single tool in a group renders exactly as today (no tabs) — tabs only when >1.

**Reuse, not reinvent:** each tab embeds the already-built widget for that tool's `input_mode`. `CompoundTabbedInput` is a *container*, not a new input type. This composes cleanly with the typed-input plan's `PathAutocompleteInput`/`MultiValueInput`.

## §D5 Problem A — submission & binding

The compound submit returns `{ outputs: { <output_var>: <value>, ... } }` covering every tab. The server's existing compound collection (`_handle_conversation_tools`, the 2205-2323 block) already iterates tools and binds `output_vars`; it routes each value through `finalize_input_value` (from the typed-input plan) — so multi-value/path/composite semantics are handled in one chokepoint (R4). No new binding logic; we extend the existing compound collector to read the per-tab `outputs` map.

## §D6 Verified facts (consolidated)

See §A1. The load-bearing facts: **B** = no `message_id` anywhere in the emit hub (B-F2/F3/F4); **A** = compound metadata exists server-side for same-round multi-tool (A-F1) but no UI renderer (A-F4) and no grouping field (A-F3); **C** = turn dir advances per loop-iteration with a `+1` anchor mismatch (C-F4/F5/F6/F7).

## §D7 Problem C — one turn directory per user message

**Decision:** a `turn_NNN/` directory represents **one user message and its complete assistant response** (including all intermediate agentic-loop iterations / widget handoffs). Loop iterations within a response are sub-structure (the `RovoDevCliInferencer-*` round dirs + the per-round `message_id` from §D1), **not** new turns.

**Two fixes:**

1. **C-bug-1 (anchor mismatch) — make the response co-locate with its own user input.** The streaming `cache_folder` and `save_turn_data` must use the **same** turn number. The response to user-turn N belongs in `turn_N/`, alongside `turn_N/turn.json`. The current `new_turn + 1` rotation (conversation_service.py:624) is the bug.

2. **C-bug-2 (granularity) — advance the turn only on a genuine new user message.** `_on_new_turn` currently fires on every agentic-loop boundary (pre-loop init + each widget handoff, C-F6). The turn directory must advance once per user message; **intra-response widget handoffs keep the same turn directory** (their rounds are distinguished by `message_id`, §D1, and by the per-round `RovoDevCliInferencer-*` subfolders). 

**Approach (least-surprise):** decouple the two responsibilities currently fused in `_on_new_turn`:
- *Per-iteration JSONL/round logging* (steps 1-3) may still fire per iteration, but write into the **current** turn dir (no `+1`).
- *Turn-directory advancement* (step 4) happens only when a **new user message** starts a turn — i.e. driven by the websocket "user sent a message" entrypoint (`manager_websocket_routes`), not by the inferencer's internal loop boundary.

This aligns the file layout with the user's mental model (1 message ⇒ 1 turn) and with the UI's turn-counting (C-F3). It also composes with §D1: within one `turn_N/`, multiple `message_id`-keyed rounds render as multiple assistant bubbles (Problem B), and grouped tools render as tabs (Problem A).

